# Resolution
Fixed in commit `92a8263b03d7d037f50d0af75433e56eb8532fe8` (`Limit multipart range verification to returned segments`).

The fix removes the verify-only segment inclusion mode from multipart range planning, so range reads now queue only the segments that overlap the requested byte range. Multipart range reads use the normal stored segment CRC checks, matching standard range reads and avoiding whole-part read/decrypt/checksum amplification.

Full multipart object reads and `GetObjectPart` full-part reads still enable the part payload CRC exit check, so the original checksum hardening remains in place where the response covers the complete stored part payload.

# Summary
Introduced an availability bug while adding multipart part checksum verification on reads. The integrity hardening verifies whole multipart parts even for tiny range requests, creating a network-triggerable resource amplification vector.
For multipart objects, `from_multipart_range` calls `multipart_ranges_for_range`, which now passes `include_verify_only_segments = true` into `segment_slices_for_range`. In that mode the slice builder no longer stops after the requested range and pushes every non-empty segment in any overlapping multipart part. Segments outside the requested byte range are represented with `(start_offset, end_offset) = (0, 0)`, so they emit no response bytes, but `SegmentListReader::next_chunk` still reads each segment and updates the part CRC verifier before discarding it. Because `MultipartReader` now always installs `expected_crc64` for each part, this verification path is active for multipart range responses. As a result, an attacker who can read an object, including anonymous users for public-read buckets, can issue very small Range GETs against large multipart parts and force the server to read/decrypt/checksum up to the whole part or object before the response completes. With the repository's 5 GiB object limit, repeated low-bandwidth requests can consume storage I/O, CPU, and blocking worker capacity disproportionately.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm multipart range construction passes `include_verify_only_segments=true`. - [x] Confirm that mode queues non-overlapping segments as zero-length output slices instead of stopping at the requested end. - [x] Dynamically demonstrate tiny-range amplification with a targeted unit-test PoC and non-multipart control. - [x] Confirm the runtime reads/checksums every queued slice before applying output offsets and that multipart readers enable expected CRC verification. - [ ] Full end-to-end HTTP GET against a large live object was not run due storage/server setup cost; validation was performed at the library planning/reader-code level with a debugger trace.
## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/availability issue. I added a targeted debug unit-test PoC that constructs one multipart part split into four 1024-byte segments and requests only byte range 1..1. The test output shows the amplification: `multipart tiny range: bytes_returned=1 slices=4 verify_only_zero_len=3 bytes_queued_for_crc=4096`, while the non-multipart control shows `bytes_returned=1 slices=1 bytes_queued_for_crc=1024`. LLDB confirmed the PoC variables at the breakpoint: `bytes_returned = 1`, `bytes_queued_for_crc = 4096`, `zero_length_verify_only = 3`, and the test exited successfully. Code evidence matches the suspected chain: `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read_core.rs:199-226` queues all non-empty segments when `include_verify_only_segments` is true and assigns non-overlapping segments `(start_offset,end_offset)=(0,0)`; `read_core.rs:261-270` passes `true` for multipart range reads; `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:1750-1760` reads each queued segment and updates the CRC before applying slice offsets; `runtime.rs:1808-1819` sets `expected_crc64: Some(...)` and `expected_verified_size` for each multipart part, activating whole-part verification. I also checked the introducing commit diff, which adds the include-verify-only mode and multipart part CRC verification. A direct crash was not expected or observed because this is a resource-amplification bug, not a memory-safety crash. Valgrind was unavailable (`command not found`), and ASan could not be used because stable Rust rejected `-Z sanitizer=address`.

