# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Severity remains medium. The finding is not a false positive: repository code shows GetObject Range reaches multipart range planning, planning queues zero-output verify-only segments, and the runtime reads/decrypts/checksums each queued segment before discarding output. The affected path is the main runtime S3 server and is reachable by a normal object reader, including anonymous users for public-read objects. The impact is meaningful transient service-level DoS/resource amplification, but it is limited to availability, requires read access to suitable multipart data, has no auth bypass or data compromise, is bounded by the 5 GiB object/part cap and request/connection semaphores, and repository defaults bind to localhost rather than proving public internet exposure.
## Likelihood
high - The exploit path is simple HTTP/S3 behavior and was validated by an executable unit-test PoC showing bytes_returned=1 with bytes_queued_for_crc=4096 in a small model. However, practical exploitation requires network reachability, a readable multipart object, and usually valid read authorization unless the bucket/object is public-read. Default bind is loopback, and in-flight limits reduce but do not eliminate amplification. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - A one-byte or similarly tiny range response against a multipart object can cause reading, optional decryption, and CRC64 verification of all segments in the touched multipart part. Repeated requests can consume storage I/O, CPU, and in-flight request capacity for the argmin-s3 service. The bug does not bypass authorization and does not expose or corrupt data, so impact is availability-only.
## Assumptions
- The vulnerable component is the argmin-s3 runtime server, not test-only code.
- A realistic attacker has network access to the HTTP listener and normal read permission for at least one large multipart object, either through SigV4 credentials/presigned URL/bucket policy/object ACL or anonymously on a public-read object.
- No deployment manifests in the repository prove internet exposure; the code default is loopback 127.0.0.1:9000, but ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR can bind an externally reachable address.
- The availability impact scales with multipart part size, segment count, encryption settings, storage speed, and configured in-flight request limits.
- argmin-s3 frontend/combined/legacy-local server is running and reachable over HTTP/HTTPS
- target object uses MultipartManifest layout
- attacker can perform GetObject/Range read for the target object
- attacker sends a small HTTP Range header such as bytes=0-0 that intersects a large multipart part
## Path
Remote reader/public client
  -> HTTP GET Object + Range
  -> server-http GetObject range dispatch
  -> Coordinator::get_object_range
  -> ReadHandle::from_multipart_range
  -> segment_slices_for_range(include_verify_only_segments=true)
  -> SegmentListReader reads/decrypts/CRC-checks queued segments
  -> storage I/O + CPU + request-slot exhaustion
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:1641-1722` - GetObject handling accepts the HTTP Range header, parses it, and calls coordinator.get_object_range with attacker-controlled byte_range.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read.rs:790-828` - Coordinator::get_object_range authorizes the read and loads the full payload layout snapshot for the requested object.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read.rs:892-928` - MultipartManifest objects route range reads to ReadHandle::from_multipart_range with the resolved user range.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read_core.rs:199-226` - When include_verify_only_segments is true, segment_slices_for_range queues every non-empty segment and represents non-overlapping segments as zero-length output slices.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read_core.rs:261-270` - multipart_ranges_for_range passes include_verify_only_segments=true for multipart range reads.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:1539-1573` - read_segment_payload reads stored segment bytes from the storage node and decrypts when encryption is configured.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:1633-1760` - SegmentListReader reads each queued segment and updates CRC state before using slice offsets, so zero-length output slices still do full read/checksum work.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:1808-1819` - MultipartReader sets expected_crc64 and expected_verified_size for each part, activating full-part verification behavior.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:531-554` - Object reads require owner/ACL/public-read authorization; public-read objects can be read without authenticated identity when public ACLs are not ignored.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:180-184` - The code allows object/upload part sizes up to 5 GiB and uses 8 MiB internal segments, giving substantial amplification potential.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:101-105` - Runtime credentials are environment-configured and the default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:888-896` - The service binds the configured TCP listener address for the S3 HTTP frontend.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:530-537` - Built-in availability controls include connection and in-flight request semaphores, but these do not remove per-request read amplification.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:764-803` - Requests that cannot acquire an in-flight slot are shed with SlowDown, bounding concurrency but allowing admitted requests to perform excessive work.
## Narrative
This is a real in-scope availability vulnerability in the main S3 server. HTTP GetObject accepts attacker-controlled Range headers and dispatches to get_object_range. For MultipartManifest objects, the range path calls from_multipart_range, which invokes multipart_ranges_for_range. That function passes include_verify_only_segments=true into segment_slices_for_range; in that mode every non-empty segment in an intersected multipart part is queued, and non-overlapping segments get start_offset/end_offset of 0/0. SegmentListReader then reads the full segment payload from storage and updates the part CRC before applying those offsets, so zero-output slices still consume disk I/O, optional decryption, and CRC CPU. MultipartReader always installs expected_crc64 for each touched part, activating this path. Authorization is still enforced, so private objects require normal read access, but public-read objects can be reached anonymously. The impact is availability-only and bounded somewhat by max object size and connection/in-flight semaphores, supporting medium rather than high/critical severity.
## Controls
- SigV4 and presigned URL authentication for private objects
- Object owner, ACL, bucket policy, public-read, and public access block authorization checks
- Default listener is 127.0.0.1:9000 unless ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is changed
- Optional TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- Connection semaphore with default ARGMIN_MAX_CONNECTIONS=512
- In-flight request semaphore with default ARGMIN_MAX_INFLIGHT_REQUESTS=32
- Admission timeout returns S3 SlowDown when request slots are exhausted
- Maximum object/upload part size is capped at 5 GiB
## Blindspots
- Static-only repository review did not run a full end-to-end HTTP test against a large live multipart object.
- No Kubernetes/Terraform/Compose ingress or load balancer manifests were found in the quick repository scan, so real deployment exposure is unknown.
- Actual amplification depends on multipart part sizes, encryption mode, disk/storage-node performance, and production configuration values.
- The provided validation PoC exercised library-level planning/reader behavior rather than a production network deployment.
